/- Each generator is given by the table of its images. A table has 280 rows, each a hexadecimal
numeral of 88 digits holding 22 entries of four digits (the last row holds 18): the entry of the
point `22 * r + j` is the group of four digits of row `r` in position `j` counted from the right,
starting at zero. The table is the natural number having the rows as its digits in base `2 ^ 352`,
so that the entry of the point `i` is its field `i` of width `16`, the bits `16 * i` to
`16 * i + 15`. The kernel reads such a field by binary arithmetic in one step, whereas a lookup in
a list or an array costs one reduction for every position passed. The field is the number
`Nat.bitField 16 t i` of `TauCeti.Data.Nat.BitField`; it is read here as
`t >>> (16 * i) % 2 ^ 16`, because on a table of this size the kernel shifts about three times
faster than it divides by `2 ^ (16 * i)`.

The kernel decides a statement quantified over `Fin n` by a recursion of depth `n`, and exceeds
its stack at `n = 6156`. Every statement about all the points is therefore checked in the form
`∀ (q : Fin 81) (r : Fin 76), P (finProdFinEquiv (q, r))`, about the point `76 * q + r`, whose two
quantifiers have depths `81` and `76`, and transported along `finProdFinEquiv`.

The bodies are exposed so that importing modules can compute with the two permutations. -/

/-- The first ATLAS generator of the third Janko group, an involution of the `6156` points, with
file labels decreased by one. Entry `i` of the table is the image of the point `i`, and also its
preimage. -/
